.TH WS-EMIT 1 "September 2026" "wildlifesystems-tools" "WildlifeSystems"
.SH NAME
ws-emit \- emit sensor readings as WildlifeSystems JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ws-emit
.RI [ "common options" ]
.B \-\-
.RI < reading >
.RI [ \-\- " " < reading > ...]
.br
.B ws-emit
.BR \-\-version | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ws-emit
turns one or more readings described on the command line into a JSON array in
the WildlifeSystems reading format, and writes it to standard output.
.PP
It exists so that a sensor driver written in shell emits exactly what the C
drivers emit. Rather than reimplementing the
.BR sc\-prototype (1)
template, the field substitution, the unit spellings and the location tokens in
.BR jq (1),
a shell driver describes its readings and
.B ws-emit
produces them using the same
.B libwildlifesystems
code the C drivers use. A change in the library therefore reaches the shell
drivers and the C drivers together.
.PP
All readings in one invocation share a timestamp, since they are taken together.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
These apply to every reading.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " NAME"
Physical device model, for example
.IR raspberry_pi .
Identifies the sensor hardware, as distinct from
.BR \-\-sensor .
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " ID"
Prefix for each reading's
.IR sensor_id ,
which becomes
.IR PREFIX_<sensor> .
Typically the node serial number.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Human-readable
.I sensor_name
for every reading.
.TP
.B \-\-internal
Mark every reading as internal to the enclosure.
.SH READING OPTIONS
Each reading follows a
.B \-\-
separator.
.TP
.BI \-\-sensor " NAME"
Sensor name, for example
.IR onboard_cpu .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-measures " NAME"
Quantity measured, for example
.IR temperature .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-unit " NAME"
Unit of measurement: one of
.IR celsius ,
.IR percentage ,
.IR hPa ,
.IR Ohms .
Matching ignores case and the canonical spelling is emitted. An unrecognised
unit is an error rather than being passed through, because a misspelled unit
silently creates a second datastream for the same quantity. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-value " NUM"
The reading. Required unless
.B \-\-error
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-precision " N"
Decimal places for the value, 0 to 10. Defaults to 3.
.TP
.BI \-\-location " TOKEN"
Either
.I {{node}}
for a sensor at the node's own position, or
.I {{none}}
for one that deliberately has none.
.I {{node}}
is resolved to the node's coordinates where they are known. Omit the option to
leave the location undeclared. Explicit coordinates are not accepted here; they
belong in a sensor's configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-\-id " ID"
Explicit
.IR sensor_id ,
overriding
.BR \-\-prefix .
.TP
.BI \-\-error " MSG"
Report a failed reading:
.I error
carries the message and
.I value
is left null. A reading never carries both.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The readings were emitted.
.TP
.B 20
A usage error, an unknown unit or location, a non-numeric value, or
.B sc\-prototype
was unavailable. Nothing is written to standard output in this case.
.SH EXAMPLES
Two readings from one call, sharing a device, prefix and internal flag:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ws-emit --device raspberry_pi --internal --prefix "$SERIAL" \\
  -- --sensor onboard_cpu --measures temperature --unit celsius \\
     --value 47 --precision 0 --location '{{node}}' \\
  -- --sensor memory_used --measures memory --unit percentage \\
     --value 31 --precision 0 --location '{{none}}'
.RE
.fi
.PP
A sensor that could not be read:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ws-emit --prefix "$SERIAL" \\
  -- --sensor onboard_gpu --measures temperature --unit celsius \\
     --error 'vcgencmd not available'
.RE
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GEOLOC_FILE
Overrides the default
.I /etc/geolocation
when resolving
.IR {{node}} .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/geolocation
The node's own position, in the GeoClue static-source format, used to resolve
.IR {{node}} .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sc\-prototype (1),
.BR sensor\-onboard (1),
.BR sr (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Ed Baker <ed@ebaker.me.uk>
